        <div id="can-protein-help" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Can Protein Powder Help With Hair Loss?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sometimes, and only for one kind of hair loss. Hair is made mostly of keratin, a protein, and the cells at the base of each hair follicle divide faster than almost any others in the body. When protein or calories run short, the body prioritizes vital functions over hair growth, and more follicles than usual can move from the growing phase into the resting phase at once. A few months later those hairs fall out together. This is called telogen effluvium, and it is the hair loss most clearly tied to diet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Severe protein deficiency is uncommon in the United States, but low intake is not. The people most likely to fall short are those on very-low-calorie diets, people recovering from bariatric surgery, people losing weight quickly on GLP-1 medications such as semaglutide or tirzepatide, older adults with small appetites, people with eating disorders, and vegans or vegetarians whose diets are poorly planned. In those situations, a protein powder is a practical way to get intake back up, and our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide shows how to set a target.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What protein cannot do</h3>

          <p>
            Most hair loss is not caused by diet. Male and female pattern hair loss, called androgenetic alopecia, is driven by genetics and the hormone dihydrotestosterone (DHT), and it progresses whether or not you eat plenty of protein. Alopecia areata is autoimmune, producing patchy loss. Thyroid disease, iron deficiency, hormonal changes after pregnancy or menopause, some medications and scalp conditions cause hair loss too. None of these respond to extra protein in someone who already eats enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the first step is diagnosis, not shopping. A dermatologist can tell the difference between shedding and pattern thinning, and a doctor can check for thyroid problems, iron deficiency and other causes with simple blood tests. Proven treatments for pattern hair loss exist, including topical minoxidil and, for men, finasteride, and a dermatologist can also discuss newer options. Starting those early matters more than any supplement.
          </p>

