            4. Telogen Effluvium: Hair Loss After Dieting, Surgery or GLP-1s
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each hair follicle cycles through a long growing phase called anagen, which lasts years on the scalp, a short transition phase, and a resting phase called telogen, which lasts around three months before the hair sheds. At any time most scalp hairs are growing and a minority are resting, and losing roughly 50 to 100 hairs a day is normal. A shock to the body can push a large share of follicles into telogen at the same time, and the shedding shows up about two to three months later.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Hair Shedding by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50-100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hairs shed a day that the American Academy of Dermatology describes as normal</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-3 mo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical delay between a trigger and telogen effluvium shedding</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~3 mo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Approximate length of the resting (telogen) phase</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">6-9 mo</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common time for shedding to settle once the trigger is gone</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Common triggers include major surgery, high fever or serious illness, childbirth, severe emotional stress, starting or stopping some medications, and rapid weight loss or very low calorie and protein intake. The delay is what makes it confusing: by the time hair starts coming out in the shower, the diet or illness that caused it may be long over. Telogen effluvium does not cause baldness on its own, and once the trigger is removed, shedding usually settles and regrowth follows over months, though it can take a year to look normal again.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bariatric surgery</h3>

          <p>
            Hair shedding is common in the first months after weight-loss surgery, when calorie intake is very low and protein targets are hard to meet. Bariatric programs set daily protein goals and often recommend protein shakes to reach them, and they monitor iron, zinc and other nutrients after surgery. Follow your program's targets rather than any general number, and see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-bariatric-surgery/">protein powder for bariatric surgery</a> guide for options suited to small, frequent servings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">GLP-1 medications</h3>

          <p>
            Hair loss was reported more often with semaglutide and tirzepatide than with placebo in clinical trials, generally in a small percentage of participants and more often among those losing weight fastest. The most likely explanation is telogen effluvium from rapid weight loss and reduced intake rather than a direct drug effect, though that is still being studied. Eating enough protein while appetite is suppressed is one of the few things you can control; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-glp-1/">protein powder for GLP-1</a> guide covers how to hit a target with low appetite. Mention shedding to your prescriber.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="does-whey-cause-hair-loss" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does Whey Protein or Creatine Cause Hair Loss?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no good evidence that whey protein causes hair loss. The worry comes from gym forums and from a link people draw between bodybuilding, hormones and receding hairlines. Whey does not contain testosterone or DHT, and we found no human study showing whey raises DHT or speeds up pattern hair loss. The real risk with sports supplements is contamination: products that are illegally spiked with anabolic steroids or prohormones can accelerate pattern hair loss in people who are genetically prone to it. Choosing a powder certified by NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice guards against that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The creatine and DHT study</h3>

          <p>
            Creatine is the supplement with an actual study behind the worry. In 2009, a small trial in 20 college-age rugby players in South Africa found that after a week of creatine loading at 25 g a day, DHT levels rose by about 56 percent, and they remained about 40 percent above baseline after two weeks of a 5 g maintenance dose. Testosterone did not change. The study did not measure hair at all, and it has not been replicated.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 12-week randomized, placebo-controlled trial published in 2025, again small at 45 resistance-trained men, gave 5 g of creatine a day and found no meaningful changes in DHT or in hair measures compared with placebo. Taken together, the evidence does not show that creatine causes hair loss, but it is thin in both directions. If you have a strong family history of pattern baldness or are already treating it, it is reasonable to discuss creatine with your dermatologist, or to choose a protein powder without it, which is why every pick here is creatine-free. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-creatine/">protein powder with creatine</a> guide covers the products that include it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen is the other ingredient marketed for hair. Collagen peptides are a real protein, but they are incomplete, missing tryptophan, and we found no good-quality trials showing collagen supplements improve hair loss. If a collagen product helps you hit your protein target it is not harmful, but it is not a hair treatment, and a complete protein does more for total intake. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein powder</a> guide explains what collagen can and cannot do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="iron-biotin-zinc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Iron, Biotin and Zinc: Do Hair Vitamins Help?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Several nutrients are genuinely needed for hair growth, and deficiencies can cause shedding. The catch is that supplementing them helps only if you are actually low, and some cause harm in excess. This is why a dermatologist will usually order blood tests before recommending anything.
          </p>

