          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Iron</h3>

          <p>
            Iron deficiency is the nutrient problem most often discussed with hair loss, particularly in women with heavy periods, people who eat little meat and people after bariatric surgery. Studies on whether low ferritin without anemia causes shedding are mixed, but checking it is routine, and treating a confirmed deficiency is worthwhile for its own sake. Do not take iron supplements without a test; excess iron is harmful, and overdoses are dangerous for children.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Biotin</h3>

          <p>
            Biotin is the most heavily marketed hair vitamin, and the evidence for it is weakest. Biotin deficiency is rare, and there is no good evidence that biotin helps hair in people who are not deficient. Hair supplements often contain 5,000 to 10,000 mcg, against an adequate intake for adults of 30 mcg. The FDA has warned that high-dose biotin can interfere with some lab tests, including tests for heart attacks and thyroid function, so tell your doctor if you take it. Protein powders marketed for hair, skin and nails sometimes add biotin; check the label.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Zinc</h3>

          <p>
            Low zinc is associated with hair loss, and people with digestive conditions, after bariatric surgery or on very restricted diets can run low. Supplementing beyond your needs is not helpful, and long-term intakes above the adult upper limit of 40 mg a day can cause copper deficiency, which brings its own problems. Food sources and a test are the sensible route; pumpkin seeds, meat and dairy all contribute.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Much Protein Do You Need to Stop Hair Shedding?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There is no hair-specific protein target. The recommended dietary allowance for adults is 0.8 g per kilogram of body weight a day, and that is the floor for avoiding deficiency, not an optimal intake. Many researchers suggest 1.0 to 1.2 g per kilogram for older adults, and higher intakes, around 1.2 to 1.6 g per kilogram, during weight loss to preserve muscle. For a 70 kg adult, that is about 56 g a day at the minimum and 85 to 110 g during a diet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spread it out. Getting roughly 25 to 30 g of protein at each meal is easier on appetite than one large serving and supports muscle across the day. If you are eating very little because of surgery or a GLP-1, it can help to use smaller servings of a powder several times a day, mixed into milk, yogurt or soup, rather than forcing one big shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calories matter as much as protein. Telogen effluvium after dieting is often a sign of losing weight too fast overall, not just of low protein. If you are losing more than about 1 percent of body weight a week for a sustained period, or eating well under 1,200 calories a day without medical supervision, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian about slowing down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then be patient. Because of the two- to three-month delay, shedding can continue for weeks after you fix your diet, and regrowth takes months to become visible. Judge whether a change worked over six months or so, not six weeks, and take photos in the same light to compare. If shedding continues beyond six months, or you see patches, scarring, scalp redness or a widening part, see a dermatologist. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> gives a personal daily target.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Buying Protein Powder for Hair Loss on Amazon: Avoiding the Hair-Claim Traps
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search Amazon for protein powder for hair and you will find products built around the claim: collagen with biotin, hair, skin and nails blends, and powders loaded with vitamins. The checks below help you buy a plain, effective protein instead of a marketing story.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ignore the hair claims in titles and bullets</h3>

          <p>
            Listing titles and bullet copy are written for search, and phrases like supports thicker hair are structure and function claims that do not need trial evidence for the finished product. Go to the listing images and find the Supplement Facts or Nutrition Facts panel. Check the protein per serving, the protein source and whether biotin, zinc, vitamin A or selenium are added and in what amounts. If you already take a multivitamin or a hair supplement, stacking another source of those nutrients can push you over safe limits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch for creatine and size or flavor variations</h3>

          <p>
            Several big brands now sell a version of their whey with creatine added, often in the same listing as the standard product, and the variation picker is the only difference. If you want to avoid creatine, check the product name on the label image, not just the thumbnail. The price shown is also often for a different size than the one pictured, so select the exact size and flavor before you compare.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Seller, certification and dates</h3>

          <p>
            Buy from listings sold by the brand or by Amazon. Third-party sellers raise the risk of counterfeits, and counterfeit or adulterated sports supplements are exactly where hidden anabolic ingredients turn up. If a product is NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice, check the lot number on the tub you receive against the certifier's database. When it arrives, confirm the seal is intact and the best-by date is well in the future.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Subscribe and Save and cost per 25 g</h3>

          <p>
            Telogen effluvium recovery takes months, so you may be buying several tubs. Subscribe and Save typically takes 5 percent off, rising to as much as 15 percent with five or more subscriptions in a month. To compare products, work out cost per 25 g of protein: divide the price by the total grams of protein in the tub (servings multiplied by protein per serving) and multiply by 25. Collagen hair blends often look cheap per tub but cost far more per 25 g of complete protein. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> does it for you.
          </p>
        </div>

