        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting protein to reverse pattern baldness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Androgenetic alopecia is driven by genetics and DHT, not diet. Extra protein will not slow it in someone who already eats enough. Treatments such as minoxidil and finasteride work best when started early, so see a dermatologist rather than waiting to see whether a powder helps.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Megadosing biotin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Biotin deficiency is rare, and there is no good evidence biotin helps people who are not deficient. Hair products often contain hundreds of times the adequate intake, and the FDA warns high doses can distort lab tests, including heart and thyroid tests. Check every supplement you take for hidden biotin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking iron or zinc without a test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both can help when you are deficient and both cause harm in excess. Too much iron is toxic and too much zinc can cause copper deficiency. Ask your doctor to check ferritin and other levels before starting supplements, especially after bariatric surgery.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying uncertified gym supplements</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Products spiked with anabolic steroids or prohormones can accelerate pattern hair loss in people prone to it. Choose NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice products, buy from the brand or Amazon directly, and check the lot number in the certifier's database.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Giving up after six weeks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Telogen effluvium shows up two to three months after its trigger, and shedding can continue for weeks after you fix the cause. Regrowth takes months to see. Judge progress over about six months, and see a dermatologist if shedding persists beyond that.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can protein powder help with hair loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only if your hair loss is linked to low protein or calorie intake, such as telogen effluvium after crash dieting, bariatric surgery or rapid weight loss. In that case, a complete protein powder can help you reach your target. It will not help pattern baldness or alopecia areata, and it will not change anything if you already eat enough protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does whey protein cause hair loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is no good evidence that it does. Whey does not contain hormones, and no human study has shown it raises DHT. The real risk is contaminated supplements spiked with anabolic ingredients, which can speed pattern hair loss in people prone to it. A third-party certified whey avoids that risk.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does creatine cause hair loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The evidence does not show that it does. A small 2009 study found creatine raised DHT in rugby players but did not measure hair, and it has not been replicated. A small 2025 trial found no meaningful change in DHT or hair measures. If you are worried or treating pattern hair loss, discuss it with your dermatologist.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What protein deficiency causes hair loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not eating enough total protein, often alongside too few calories, can push many hair follicles into their resting phase at once, causing telogen effluvium about two to three months later. Severe deficiency can also make hair thin, dry and brittle. It is most likely after surgery, crash dieting, eating disorders or rapid weight loss.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is collagen good for hair loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  There is no good-quality trial evidence that collagen supplements treat hair loss. Collagen counts toward your protein intake, but it is incomplete, lacking tryptophan, so it is a weaker way to fix a protein shortfall than whey, egg or a complete plant blend. Treat hair claims on collagen products as marketing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How long does it take for hair to grow back after protein deficiency?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Shedding usually settles within about six to nine months after the cause is fixed, and visible regrowth follows over several more months because scalp hair grows only around a centimeter a month. If shedding continues beyond six months or you see patches or scalp changes, see a dermatologist for assessment.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Should I take biotin with protein powder for hair?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not unless a doctor finds you are deficient, which is rare. Biotin has no proven benefit for hair in people with normal levels, and high doses can interfere with lab tests. If your protein powder or multivitamin already contains biotin, avoid adding a separate hair supplement and tell your doctor before blood tests.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
